  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The kitchen staff is very busy preparing food for the evening guests. One of the cooks suddenly realizes that a dish he just prepared has mysteriously disappeared from his table.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Привет. Где мой салат?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Я его не брал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Утром он был здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Спроси у других поваров.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Уи, ты мой салат не видел?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Нет, я готовил десерты.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Куда он мог пропасть?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Может, его уже подали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это был мой лучший салат.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Сделай новый, время есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Гости будут ждать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ничего страшного не случится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не успею сделать новый.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тогда придумай что-нибудь другое.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Уи, ты мой салат не видел?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hey, have you seen my salad?' In Russian, 'oui' leads into 'saw' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это был мой лучший салат.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It was my best salad.' Notice the word order: 'this' comes first, and 'salad' finishes the sentence. Then Fedya replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Сделай новый, время есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Make a new one, there is time.' The first content word means 'make', and 'there is' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не успею сделать новый.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will not have time to make a new one.' You'll hear 'I' all the time in the show, here connected with 'new'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тогда придумай что-нибудь другое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then think of something else.' In Russian, 'back then' leads into 'another' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
